  "assessment_summary": "This paper presents a large-scale randomized experiment studying the effects of guest worker job offers on Indian construction workers migrating to the UAE. The study is ambitious in scope, examining not just earnings but also well-being, labor intermediary costs, social networks, attitudes, and financial outcomes. The experimental design — randomizing job offers among workers who passed a skills screening at recruitment sites — is clever and well-suited to the policy question. The paper makes several important contributions: it is among the first to provide experimental evidence on the individual-level costs and benefits of guest worker programs (as opposed to household-level effects), it documents the role and cost structure of labor intermediaries, and it finds that substantial non-pecuniary costs (physical toll, heat, reduced well-being) help explain why roughly half of offered workers decline migration despite large pecuniary returns.\n\nSeveral methodological concerns deserve attention. First, attrition is a significant issue. While the overall follow-up rate (~66%) is comparable to similar studies, there is differential attrition between treatment and control groups, and attriters differ from non-attriters on observables (education, caste, ability score). The authors address this through inverse probability weighting and bounding exercises, but the well-being results are sensitive to the bounding assumptions — the upper bound on well-being becomes insignificant when attritors are assumed to be more than 15% of a standard deviation from the mean. This is a meaningful limitation for the well-being finding, which is one of the paper's headline results.\n\nSecond, the paper did not pre-specify regressions or outcome variables despite pre-registering the experiment (acknowledged in footnote 29). Given the large number of outcomes examined across multiple domains (labor market, well-being, work satisfaction, financial, attitudes, social networks), this raises concerns about selective reporting and multiple hypothesis testing. No formal adjustment for multiple comparisons is presented.\n\nThird, the baseline balance shows a significant difference in net assets between treatment and control (p=0.03 unconditionally, p=0.03 conditionally). While this is only one variable out of many, net assets are economically important and could correlate with migration decisions and outcomes.\n\nFourth, the marginal treatment effects analysis relies heavily on the linear MTE assumption (following Brinch et al. 2017), which is acknowledged as necessary given only a binary instrument. The extrapolated outcomes for never-takers in the UAE and always-takers in India are driven entirely by this functional form assumption and should be interpreted cautiously. The confidence intervals on the never-taker UAE outcomes are quite wide.\n\nFifth, the efficiency calculation in Section 4.8, which argues that doubling UAE wages would induce 100% take-up and increase aggregate efficiency, relies on combining estimates from this paper with external estimates (firm-specific recruitment elasticities from Naidu et al. 2016, income-well-being relationships from McGuire et al. 2022 and Lindqvist et al. 2020). The calculation assumes the linear MTE extrapolations are correct, that agent fees and non-income well-being changes would remain constant under doubled wages, and that never-takers would be paid the (lower) MTE-extrapolated wage rather than the observed wage. These are strong assumptions that could substantially affect the conclusions.\n\nSixth, while the paper provides rich descriptive evidence on labor intermediaries (Table 1), the causal interpretation of agent fee effects relies on the assumption that the randomized offer only affects agent fees through increasing migration. This seems reasonable but isn't formally tested.\n\nSeventh, the external validity of the findings is limited to construction workers at two large firms. The authors argue these firms are representative based on modal salary comparisons (Appendix Figure A.5), but other important dimensions of job quality (safety, management, working conditions) cannot be verified from salary data alone. The authors themselves note that large firms may be more likely to follow labor regulations.\n\nOn the positive side, the paper's strengths are substantial. The randomized design is well-implemented and provides clean causal estimates for the ITT effects. The rich data collection across multiple survey rounds, combined with administrative data from the UAE MOL, allows for comprehensive analysis and robustness checks. The examination of expectations versus realizations, reservation wages, and MTE heterogeneity provides novel insights into migration decisions. The finding that non-pecuniary costs — particularly physical demands and climate — rather than social isolation or loneliness drive the well-being decline is an important contribution. The documentation of labor intermediary costs and their contingent payment structure is valuable. The paper is well-written, clearly organized, and situates its findings carefully within the existing literature.\n\nThe paper's policy implications — that improving working conditions may be more effective than reducing recruitment fees in expanding guest worker migration — are important and supported by the evidence, though the specific efficiency calculations should be taken as illustrative rather than definitive.",
